# Cloudflare Pages headers file (https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/).
# Everything in `public/` is copied verbatim into the build output, so this
# file ships as-is to Cloudflare Pages — no build step touches it.
#
# REQ-001-A2 (E14/U15) — X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy and
# Content-Security-Policy used to be declared here (R-7.5). They are NOT
# anymore, and must never be added back here while functions/_middleware.ts
# exists.
#
# Why: Cloudflare's edge injects an inline JavaScript Detections / Bot
# Fight Mode bootstrap script into every proxied HTML response on this
# zone, which a static, nonce-less `script-src 'self'` cannot authorize
# without weakening it to `'unsafe-inline'` (QA-005 Finding 1). A nonce has
# to be minted per-request, which a static file cannot do — only a Pages
# Function can, so `functions/_middleware.ts` is now the sole authoritative
# source for these three headers (REQ-001-A2 R-7.5 AC1's clarifying note).
#
# A static `_headers` block and the Function are two independent mechanisms
# capable of emitting the same header name on the same response, with no
# established, documented Cloudflare precedence rule between them (REQ-001-A2
# U17). Rather than lean on an assumed "one wins" behavior, this file simply
# never declares these three headers at all, so there is only ever one
# source, structurally, not by discipline. See
# tests/security-headers.spec.ts's guard test, which fails the build if any
# of the three ever reappear here — that test is the enforcement mechanism
# this comment is not.
#
# Nothing else has ever needed a header from this file (no other route
# pattern, no other header name has been declared here to date), so this
# file currently declares nothing. It stays in the repo, not deleted,
# because Cloudflare Pages' own `_headers` mechanism is still the right tool
# for any FUTURE header that isn't request-time-dependent (i.e. doesn't need
# a nonce or other per-response value) — deleting the file would just get
# rewritten the moment such a need arises. Whoever adds a header here next
# must re-read this file's history: never add CSP / X-Content-Type-Options /
# Referrer-Policy back here while the Function owns them.
